No `.csproj` found, so I can't confirm the target framework. Since this is a **library with no DI**, the lightest approach is the **ambient context pattern** using `TimeProvider` (built-in on .NET 8+). This avoids adding a DI container entirely.

Here's the recommended approach:

**1. Add a static `Clock` class to your library:**

```csharp
namespace InvoiceLib;

public static class Clock
{
    private static readonly AsyncLocal<Func<DateTimeOffset>?> s_override = new();

    public static DateTimeOffset UtcNow
        => s_override.Value?.Invoke() ?? TimeProvider.System.GetUtcNow();

    /// <summary>Test-only: pin the clock to a fixed time.</summary>
    public static IDisposable Override(DateTimeOffset fixedTime)
    {
        s_override.Value = () => fixedTime;
        return new Scope();
    }

    private sealed class Scope : IDisposable
    {
        public void Dispose() => s_override.Value = null;
    }
}
```

**2. Replace `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow` with `Clock.UtcNow`:**

```csharp
public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
    => Clock.UtcNow > dueDate;
```

**3. In tests, pin the time:**

```csharp
using var _ = Clock.Override(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 3, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
Assert.True(calculator.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 28, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this works well for a library:**

- No DI container needed — one-line change per call site
- `AsyncLocal<T>` keeps parallel tests isolated
- Production cost is a single null-check per call
- `IDisposable` scope ensures cleanup even if a test throws

